\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[margin=25mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\title{Excluding two odd orders by the same cycle partition}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
The preceding component~\cite{latin} excludes a graph of parameters
$(18,6,4,12)$, but square divisibility alone permits odd orders.
We complete the corresponding lower bound in Table~4 of
Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ}.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
A finite simple graph of degree six, girth four and twelve squares
through every edge has at least twenty vertices.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The acquired tree bound of Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman,
retained as equation~(2) of~\cite{bounds}, gives
\[
 n\ge2(1+5)+\left\lceil\frac{2(5^2-12)}6\right\rceil=17.
\]
The eighteen-vertex case is excluded by~\cite{latin}. It remains
to exclude seventeen and nineteen.

A regular bipartite graph of positive degree has parts of equal
size, so neither odd order is bipartite. The shortest-odd-cycle
inequality $6\ell\le2n$ forces a five-cycle at both orders.
Use exactly the cycle-neighbourhood partition of~\cite{latin}:
$a_i$ vertices have cycle neighbours $v_{i-1},v_{i+1}$,
$b_i$ have only $v_i$, and $z$ have none. The cycle vertices
are included in the $a_i$ classes. The degree equations are
\[
 a_{i-1}+a_{i+1}+b_i=6,\qquad
 \sum_i b_i+2z=2n-30,\qquad a_i\ge1,\quad b_i,z\ge0.
\]
Thus the right-hand side is four or eight. Enumerate its weak
five-part compositions after subtracting $2z$, solve for $a$ by
the explicit linear formulas in~\cite{latin}, and retain one
representative under rotation and reversal. This gives fourteen
patterns at order seventeen and forty-two at order nineteen.
Independent enumeration of all positive $a$-vectors gives the
same complete list of labelled class patterns.

The degree-completion algorithm and its full branching proof are
those of~\cite{latin}, with the single target value twelve and the
new class patterns. The proof does not depend on order eighteen:
all cycle degrees are closed initially, the opposite neighbourhoods
of the five cycle edges remain fixed, and each subsequent step
assigns precisely the outstanding neighbours of one vertex.
The local triangle test and the lower and upper bounds on cycle-edge
square counts remain necessary in both orders. No new mathematical
prune is introduced here.

The exact computations complete every pattern and return no graph:
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
\text{Order}&\text{Patterns}&\text{Visited nodes}&\text{Retained graphs}\\\hline
17&14&9\,111&0\\
19&42&9\,502\,832&0
\end{array}
\]
The accompanying verifier recomputes every case. At each terminal state, degrees,
triangle-freeness, and the square count on every edge are checked.
This excludes both remaining orders and proves the theorem.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{The implementation and its finite controls.}
A second implementation uses unsigned adjacency masks in C++.
All indices lie between zero and eighteen, so a 32-bit mask contains
every vertex. The set operations in the acquired branching proof
are replaced by mask intersection, deletion and population counts.
The selected vertex minimizes, in order, the number of available
neighbours minus its deficit, the number of available neighbours,
and its label. A recursive subset iterator chooses the prescribed
number of available neighbours in increasing order. It discards a
choice only if it contains an edge, precisely the acquired triangle
condition. Degrees and adjacency masks are restored after each return.

For each original cycle edge, summing populations of adjacency
intersections gives its present square count. Summing the candidate
intersections gives an upper bound on missing future contributions.
The bit masks therefore implement the same two inequalities as the
Python program; they introduce neither heuristic graph equivalence
nor rounding. At a completed graph, the all-edge square predicate
is applied independently of the five initial bounds. A timeout is
reported as incomplete and cannot certify an exclusion.

The C++ implementation is compared with the independent Python
implementation on all twenty-seven order-eighteen patterns: every
individual node count agrees, for a total of $772\,757$.
The smaller-order controls include the balanced blow-up of a
five-cycle at order fifteen, whose constant square count is sixteen;
it is found as a positive completion. They also include all four
order-sixteen patterns, the fourteen order-seventeen patterns, and
selected small Python cases at order nineteen. Every corresponding
completed result and node count agrees. The ordinary and address-
and undefined-behaviour-sanitized C++ executions are compared on
the full inventories, including all forty-two cases at order nineteen.
These comparisons supplement the branching proof; a failed or
unfinished comparison is never treated as evidence of nonexistence.

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
The lower bound is a prior result of~\cite{GJ}, Table~4, physical
page~20. The general tree bound remains attributed to its original
authors; its complete acquired proof is a premise, not the F012
table entry itself. The cycle partition and complete degree-completion
argument are reused from~\cite{latin}, which in turn records the
origin of its recursion in the earlier independent order-sixteen
control. The present contribution closes two additional finite orders
and supplies a faster exact implementation of the same search.
It proves no upper bound or uniqueness statement for this parameter,
and makes no open-conjecture, historical priority or Lean claim.
